{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And the angels of, etc. encountered him.</b> One needs to know the intent of the verse's saying <b>and they encountered him.</b> Also, its saying <b>And Jacob said,</b> etc. — what novelty does the verse convey by telling us that Jacob said so? Also, what did Jacob innovate in his words? Also, the intent of his saying <b>Mahanaim</b> is not known. And our Rabbis of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 78:17) interpreted it — one may review their words there, for it is a midrashic interpretation." "And the plain sense of the verse is as follows: since HaShem sent angels in the likeness of men, to appear before Esau as the end of the episode demonstrated, therefore the verse prefaced by informing of the matter through which Jacob sensed that they were angels. And it says that the camp did not come in the manner of men, who are normally seen gradually approaching, getting closer and closer, until they reach the one they are coming to; rather, they sprang up immediately right before him — and this is what it means by <b>and they encountered him</b>, meaning the very first sight was the encounter with him right there. ״And Jacob said when he saw them״ in this manner — ״a camp of Elohim״: these are not men but angels. ״And he called the name of the place Mahanaim,״ meaning two camps: one of his men who were with him to array for battle, and the second of angels in the likeness of men, for they came to help HaShem's beloved, Jacob, the chosen of HaShem." "<b>Here concludes the portion of Vayetze.</b>"], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:45:28.936932753Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}